  "title": "Open banking APIs: the integration traps",
  "content": "It works in the simple case and breaks in a specific way under load. Most nairobi teams writeups assume the reader has already made the mistake they're warning about. Here's the root cause, not just the symptom.\n\nMost teams treat open banking integration as a REST API problem: read the spec, wire up OAuth, map the fields, ship. That mental model is wrong, and it is wrong in a way that only becomes visible after launch, when a payment initiation that passed in one market fails in another for reasons that have nothing to do with your code. The contrarian take here is simple: **open banking is not one API with regional quirks; it is a family of incompatible contracts that happen to share a name.** The integration differences between markets are not edge cases to handle later. They are the product. If you design for the lowest common denominator and patch per market, you will spend the rest of the project patching.\n\nThis post is about the specific, documented differences that catch teams out: consent models, authentication flows, payload semantics, error taxonomies, and the operational realities of sandboxes that do not behave like production. It is not a market-by-market catalogue, because those change too fast to be useful in a blog post. It is a set of failure modes and a decision framework so you can ask the right questions before you write the first line of integration code.\n\n## The conventional wisdom (and why it's incomplete)\n\nThe standard advice goes something like this: adopt a standard (Berlin Group, UK Open Banking, FDX, or a local variant), use an aggregator or a gateway that normalises across markets, and keep your domain model clean by translating everything into your own canonical schema. That advice is not wrong. It is incomplete in three specific ways.\n\nFirst, **the standards are not interchangeable at the semantic level**. Two APIs can both claim to implement \"account information\" and still disagree on what a balance means: available vs. booked, whether pending transactions are included, how overdrafts are represented, and whether an account identifier is stable across consents. A canonical schema that flattens these differences will be wrong in at least one market, and you will not know which until a customer disputes a balance.\n\nSecond, **consent is not a checkbox; it is a state machine with legal weight**. In the UK, consent expires and must be re-authenticated; in other regimes, consent duration, revocation semantics, and what you are allowed to do with the data after revocation differ. If your integration treats consent as a token you refresh like any other, you will build something that works in sandbox and fails audit.\n\nThird, **the error model is part of the contract**. A `400` in one market might mean \"malformed request\" and in another \"consent expired\". Retry logic that treats all 4xx as non-retryable will drop payments that should have been retried after re-consent; retry logic that treats all 5xx as retryable will double-submit in markets where the payment initiation is not idempotent by default.\n\nThe conventional wisdom assumes the hard part is authentication and data mapping. The hard part is that the same verb means different things in different markets, and the documentation rarely says so explicitly.\n\n## What actually happens when you follow the standard advice\n\nA typical failure mode is the \"works in sandbox, fails in production\" gap. Sandboxes are often more permissive than production: they may accept consent without a real redirect, return synthetic data with stable identifiers, and never simulate partial failures. Teams commonly build against the sandbox, pass integration tests, and then discover in production that:\n\n- **Consent expiry is enforced differently.** A token that the sandbox refreshes indefinitely may, in production, require the user to be present for re-authentication after a fixed period. Your background job that syncs transactions nightly will start failing silently with a consent error that your code maps to a generic `401`.\n- **Rate limits are per-consent, not per-client.** You may have a generous global quota but a much tighter per-account limit, so a batch job that fans out across many accounts hits `429` on a subset and your retry logic thundering-herds.\n- **Idempotency keys are not universal.** Some payment initiation endpoints honour an idempotency key; others do not, or only for a limited window. A network timeout followed by a retry can create a duplicate payment. This is the single most expensive integration bug in open banking, and it is documented in some markets and glossed over in others.\n- **Webhook delivery is not guaranteed.** Some markets rely on polling; others offer webhooks with at-least-once delivery and no ordering guarantee. If your state machine assumes ordered events, you will process a \"settled\" before a \"pending\" and corrupt your ledger.\n\nThe pattern is that the standard advice optimises for code reuse across markets. The failure mode is that code reuse hides semantic differences until they become financial discrepancies.\n\n## A different mental model\n\nInstead of thinking \"one integration, many configurations\", think **\"one core, many adapters, and a market capability matrix that is a first-class artefact.\"** The core is your domain logic: what a payment is, what a consent authorises, how you reconcile. The adapters are per-market and are allowed to be ugly. The capability matrix is the thing that keeps you honest: for each market, you record explicitly which features are supported, which are emulated, and which are impossible.\n\nA capability matrix is not a spreadsheet you fill in once. It is a set of assertions you encode in code and test. For example, a minimal TypeScript type that forces you to declare, per market, whether idempotency and consent refresh are supported:\n\n```typescript\ntype MarketCapabilities = {\n  idempotentPaymentInitiation: boolean;\n  consentRefreshRequiresUserPresence: boolean;\n  webhookDelivery: 'guaranteed' | 'at-least-once' | 'none';\n  balanceSemantics: 'available' | 'booked' | 'both';\n  errorModel: 'standardised' | 'market-specific';\n};\n\nconst uk: MarketCapabilities = {\n  idempotentPaymentInitiation: true,\n  consentRefreshRequiresUserPresence: true,\n  webhookDelivery: 'at-least-once',\n  balanceSemantics: 'both',\n  errorModel: 'standardised',\n};\n\nconst marketX: MarketCapabilities = {\n  idempotentPaymentInitiation: false,\n  consentRefreshRequiresUserPresence: false,\n  webhookDelivery: 'none',\n  balanceSemantics: 'available',\n  errorModel: 'market-specific',\n};\n\nfunction initiatePayment(market: MarketCapabilities, request: PaymentRequest) {\n  if (!market.idempotentPaymentInitiation) {\n    // fall back to a client-side deduplication key stored before the call\n    return initiateWithClientDedup(request);\n  }\n  return initiateWithIdempotencyKey(request);\n}\n```\n\nThis is deliberately not clever. The point is that the difference is visible at the call site, not buried in a configuration flag that someone flips without understanding the consequence.\n\nThe mental model shift also changes how you evaluate aggregators and gateways. An aggregator that claims to normalise across markets is really offering you its capability matrix, not yours. That can be a good trade, but only if you know which differences it has flattened and whether those flattenings are lossy for your use case. If you need to know whether a balance includes pending transactions, and the aggregator's canonical model says \"balance\", you have outsourced a decision you may not be able to audit.\n\n## Documented evidence and typical examples\n\nYou do not need to trust a blog post for this; the differences are in the specifications. The UK Open Banking Standard (part of the Open Banking Implementation Entity, now run under Open Banking Limited) defines consent as a resource with an explicit expiry and a `Status` that moves through `AwaitingAuthorisation`, `Authorised`, `Rejected`, `Revoked`, and `Expired`. The Berlin Group NextGenPSD2 framework defines consent similarly but with different endpoint shapes and error codes. The FDX API in the US has its own consent and data-sharing model. These are real, publicly documented standards, and they do not share a single consent lifecycle.\n\nA concrete example that trips teams: **the meaning of a `401` versus a `403`**. In OAuth 2.0, `401` means the access token is missing or invalid; `403` means the token is valid but lacks scope. Open banking APIs generally follow this, but some return `401` for an expired consent and expect the client to re-initiate the consent flow, while others return `403` with a specific error code that means the same thing. If your HTTP client has a global rule \"on 401, refresh token\", you will attempt a token refresh that cannot succeed because the consent, not the token, is the problem. The fix is to map error codes to domain actions per market, not to rely on HTTP status alone.\n\nAnother documented difference is **payment initiation idempotency**. Some standards define an `x-idempotency-key` header or equivalent; others rely on a client-generated `PaymentId` that must be unique. If you assume the header is honoured everywhere, a retry after a timeout can create a second payment. The mitigation is not clever code; it is a pre-call deduplication record with a unique constraint in your own database, written before the network call and reconciled after. Here is the shape of that, in Python, using a unique constraint to make the deduplication safe under concurrency:\n\n```python\nimport uuid\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine, Column, String, DateTime, UniqueConstraint\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base, sessionmaker\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n\nBase = declarative_base()\n\nclass PaymentAttempt(Base):\n    __tablename__ = 'payment_attempts'\n    id = Column(String, primary_key=True)\n    client_dedup_key = Column(String, nullable=False)\n    market = Column(String, nullable=False)\n    status = Column(String, nullable=False, default='initiated')\n    created_at = Column(DateTime, default=lambda: datetime.now(timezone.utc))\n    __table_args__ = (UniqueConstraint('client_dedup_key', 'market', name='uq_dedup'),)\n\nengine = create_engine('sqlite:///payments.db')\nBase.metadata.create_all(engine)\nSession = sessionmaker(bind=engine)\n\ndef initiate_payment(market: str, dedup_key: str, payload: dict):\n    session = Session()\n    attempt_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n    try:\n        session.add(PaymentAttempt(id=attempt_id, client_dedup_key=dedup_key, market=market))\n        session.commit()\n    except Exception:\n        session.rollback()\n        # A row already exists for this dedup key: return the existing attempt\n        existing = session.query(PaymentAttempt).filter_by(\n            client_dedup_key=dedup_key, market=market).one()\n        return existing.id\n    # Only now make the network call\n    result = call_market_api(market, payload, idempotency_key=dedup_key)\n    attempt = session.query(PaymentAttempt).get(attempt_id)\n    attempt.status = 'submitted'\n    session.commit()\n    return attempt_id\n```\n\nThis is illustrative and simplified (it omits error handling and the reconciliation job), but the principle is the point: the database constraint, not the remote API, is what makes retries safe. That works even in markets where the API does not support idempotency keys.\n\n## The cases where the conventional wisdom IS right\n\nThere are situations where a single normalised integration is the correct call. If you are building a product where the financial consequence of a semantic difference is low — say, an expense categorisation tool that reads transactions but never initiates payments — then a lossy canonical model is an acceptable trade for speed. Similarly, if you are operating in a single market and have no near-term plans to expand, building a market-specific adapter is over-engineering; the capability matrix is a document you write when you add the second market.\n\nAggregators are also genuinely the right choice when your team does not have the compliance capacity to manage consent and data handling per market. The trade is real: you give up some control over semantics in exchange for not having to maintain five consent state machines. The mistake is choosing an aggregator without asking which differences it has flattened, and then discovering the answer during an audit or a customer dispute.\n\nThe conventional wisdom is right about code reuse in principle. It is wrong when it treats code reuse as the primary goal rather than a constraint to be balanced against semantic correctness.\n\n## How to decide which approach fits your situation\n\nA decision framework, in order of importance:\n\n1. **Does the integration move money?** If yes, every semantic difference is a potential financial discrepancy, and you need per-market adapters and explicit idempotency. If no, a normalised read model is usually fine.\n2. **How many markets, and how different are their consent regimes?** One market: build directly. Two or three with similar regimes: consider a shared core with thin adapters. Many markets with materially different consent laws: aggregator or a gateway, with a documented list of what it normalises.\n3. **What is your audit and dispute exposure?** If you must be able to explain to a regulator or a customer why a balance differed, you need to retain the raw response alongside your canonical model. That requirement alone rules out some aggregators that do not expose raw payloads.\n4. **What is your team's operational capacity?** Every market adapter is a thing to monitor, alert on, and update when the standard changes. A capability matrix with ten markets is ten rows of ongoing work. Be honest about whether you can staff that.\n\nThe output of this framework is not a single answer; it is a documented decision per market, which is exactly what the capability matrix encodes.\n\n## Common objections, and responses\n\n**\"This sounds like a lot of abstraction for something that is mostly CRUD.\"** It is mostly CRUD until a payment double-submits or a consent expires mid-sync. The abstraction is not there to be elegant; it is there to make the differences explicit at the point where they matter. A capability matrix is cheaper than a reconciliation incident.\n\n**\"An aggregator already solves this.\"** An aggregator solves the integration effort, not the semantic problem. It gives you its model. If that model is sufficient for your product, use it. If you need to know whether a balance includes pending items, ask the aggregator directly and get the answer in writing before you build on it.\n\n**\"We will handle market differences with configuration.\"** Configuration is fine for endpoints and credentials. It is not fine for behavioural differences like idempotency support or consent refresh semantics, because those change control flow, not just values. Encode them in types or interfaces that force a decision at the call site.\n\n**\"The standards are converging.\"** They are converging in some areas and diverging in others, and convergence takes years. You are building now. Design for the differences that exist, and you will be well placed when they narrow.\n\n## What the alternative approach would change\n\nAdopting the capability-matrix model changes three things in practice. First, your integration tests become market-specific: you test that a retry after timeout does not create a duplicate in a market without idempotency keys, not just that a happy-path payment succeeds. Second, your incident response improves because errors are mapped to domain actions per market, so an on-call engineer sees \"consent expired, re-auth required\" rather than \"401\". Third, your expansion cost becomes predictable: adding a market means filling in a matrix row and writing an adapter, not discovering a new class of bug in production.\n\nIt also changes how you talk to vendors. You stop asking \"do you support open banking?\" and start asking \"how do you represent a balance, what happens to consent on expiry, and do you honour idempotency keys on payment initiation?\" Those questions get useful answers.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe integration differences between open banking markets are not quirks to be patched; they are the core design constraint. The conventional advice to normalise everything into one model is a reasonable default for read-only products and a dangerous one for anything that moves money. The alternative is to treat per-market capabilities as a first-class artefact, encode them where they affect control flow, and make retries safe with your own database constraints rather than trusting remote idempotency.\n\n**Do this next:** open your payment initiation code and search for the place where you handle a network timeout. If the retry path does not write a deduplication record with a unique constraint before the second call, you have found the bug that will cost you the most. Add that constraint today, before you add another market.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Why does my open banking integration work in sandbox but fail in production?**\nSandboxes often relax consent expiry, return stable synthetic identifiers, and do not simulate partial failures or rate limits per consent. Production enforces these, so code paths that assume a token can be refreshed indefinitely or that every 4xx is permanent will fail. Test against documented production constraints, not just sandbox behaviour.\n\n**Do all open banking [APIs support idempotency keys](/idempotency-keys-what-they-fix-what-they-dont/) for payments?**\nNo. Some standards define an idempotency header or a client-supplied payment ID, and some markets honour neither reliably. The safe approach is to implement deduplication on your side with a unique constraint in your database, written before the network call, and reconcile afterwards. Do not assume the remote API will prevent duplicates.\n\n**How should I handle consent expiry across different markets?**\nModel consent as a state machine with an explicit expiry and a re-authentication path, and map each market's error codes to that state machine. Do not treat consent expiry as an authentication failure that a token refresh can fix. Some markets require the user to be present for re-consent, which means background sync jobs must degrade gracefully rather than retry indefinitely.\n\n**Is it better to use an aggregator or integrate directly with each bank?**\nIt depends on whether the aggregator's normalised model is sufficient for your product and whether you can audit its flattening decisions. For read-only products, an aggregator is often the right trade. For payment initiation or anything where balance semantics matter, ask the aggregator specifically how it represents balances, consent expiry, and idempotency, and get the answer in writing before you build.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
